#original-page(595.32pt, 841.92pt, (image("graphics/page-02.png", width: 456.5pt), 69.5pt, 764pt))[
  #set text(font: "Calibri", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(294.89pt, 775.896pt, "2 ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 81.36pt, "Traditional gas sensors can be categorized into six types based on their operating principles: ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 95.9pt, "thermoelectric, mass-sensitive, magnetic, electrochemical, optical, and electronic [4,6]. Among these ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 110.3pt, "categories, electronic gas sensors offer several advantages, including high sensitivity, reasonable ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 124.82pt, "selectivity, low power consumption (typically below a few milliwatts), relatively simple fabrication and ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 139.34pt, "operation, low cost, compact size, compatibility with IoT (Internet of Things) systems, and long-term ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 153.86pt, "stability [4,23–28]. Table 1 presents a comparison of different gas sensor types, mainly focusing on ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 168.26pt, "sensitivity, LOD (limit of detection) for NO")
  #set text(font: "Calibri", size: 6.96pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(262.61pt, 169.22pt, "2")
  #set text(font: "Calibri", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(266.09pt, 168.26pt, " concentration, selectivity, and energy consumption [17–")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 182.78pt, "26]. In terms of sensitivity, using NO")
  #set text(font: "Calibri", size: 6.96pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(240.89pt, 183.74pt, "2")
  #set text(font: "Calibri", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(244.37pt, 182.78pt, " as an example, thermoelectric gas sensors demonstrate the ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 197.3pt, "lowest detection capability, with a LOD of 2 ppm [9]. Mass-sensitive and magnetic gas sensors can ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 211.82pt, "detect NO")
  #set text(font: "Calibri", size: 6.96pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(118.1pt, 212.78pt, "2")
  #set text(font: "Calibri", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(121.58pt, 211.82pt, " at concentrations of 175 ppb [13] and 150 ppb [16], respectively. Electrochemical and ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 226.22pt, "optical sensors follow, achieving LODs of 4.8 ppb and 1 ppb [19,22], respectively. In contrast, electronic ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 240.74pt, "gas sensors exhibit the highest detection capability, with a theoretically estimated LOD reaching as low ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 255.26pt, "as 0.05 ppb [26]. ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 277.73pt, "In conventional electronic gas sensors, the sensing layers usually consist of semiconducting metal ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 291.17pt, "oxides such as tin, tungsten, zinc, indium, or titanium oxide, and have, depending on the deposition ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 304.61pt, "method, typical thicknesses ranging from several nm up to the µm range. For a high sensitivity and a ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 318.05pt, "small LOD, the surface-to-volume ratio of the sensing layer should be large, thus thin sensing layers ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 331.49pt, "are preferable.    ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 350.93pt, "2D (two-dimensional) materials such as semiconducting mono- or few-layer TMDCs (transition metal ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 364.37pt, "dichalcogenides) offer unmatched surface-to-volume ratios since they are ultimately thin. Monolayer ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 377.81pt, "MoS")
  #set text(font: "Calibri", size: 6.96pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(91.344pt, 378.77pt, "2")
  #set text(font: "Calibri", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(94.824pt, 377.81pt, ", for example, has a thickness of only 0.65 nm. Therefore, 2D TMDCs are considered as promising ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 391.25pt, "candidates for the sensing layer of future high-sensitivity electronic gas sensors [5,29–31].  ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 410.57pt, "Numerous experimental TMDC-based gas sensors, particularly those employing MoS")
  #set text(font: "Calibri", size: 6.96pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(465.22pt, 411.53pt, "2")
  #set text(font: "Calibri", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(468.7pt, 410.57pt, ", have been ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 425.09pt, "reported and their response to gases has been investigated [32–44]. On the theoretical side, extensive ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 439.63pt, "efforts have been devoted to ")
  #set text(font: "Calibri", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "italic", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(206.81pt, 439.63pt, "ab initio")
  #set text(font: "Calibri", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(243.65pt, 439.63pt, ", i.e., first-principles, simulations using DFT (density functional ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 454.15pt, "theory) to investigate the fundamental principles (or mechanisms) of the adsorption of gas molecules ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 468.55pt, "on TMDC surfaces and to calculate characteristic adsorption parameters such as the adsorption energy ")
  #set text(font: "Calibri", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "italic", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(70.944pt, 483.07pt, "E")
  #set text(font: "Calibri", size: 6.96pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(76.344pt, 484.03pt, "ads")
  #set text(font: "Calibri", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(86.064pt, 483.07pt, " and the resulting charge transfer Δ")
  #set text(font: "Calibri", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "italic", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(245.21pt, 483.07pt, "Q")
  #set text(font: "Calibri", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(252.41pt, 483.07pt, " [45–58]. On the other hand, limited attention has been paid ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 497.59pt, "to activities related to modeling gas adsorption on 2D materials at the device level and to describing ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 512.11pt, "the response of 2D gas sensors to the gas exposure. ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 532.51pt, "In the present work, a modeling approach to describe the electrical response of MoS")
  #set text(font: "Calibri", size: 6.96pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(472.18pt, 533.47pt, "2")
  #set text(font: "Calibri", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(475.66pt, 532.51pt, "-based gas ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 545.95pt, "sensors to ambient gas exposure is proposed. It is based on the Langmuir adsorption isotherm, ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 559.39pt, "combines essential theoretical concepts of gas adsorption on MoS")
  #set text(font: "Calibri", size: 6.96pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(364.99pt, 560.35pt, "2")
  #set text(font: "Calibri", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(368.47pt, 559.39pt, " surfaces, and subsequently relates ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 572.83pt, "the modeled sensor response to that of experimental sensors reported in the literature. The proposed ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 586.27pt, "model effectively and quantitatively bridges the gap between the first-principles investigations of gas ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 599.71pt, "adsorption and experimental results covering low and high gas concentration ranges. Validation using ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 613.15pt, "NH")
  #set text(font: "Calibri", size: 6.96pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(84.864pt, 614.11pt, "3")
  #set text(font: "Calibri", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(88.344pt, 613.15pt, " and NO")
  #set text(font: "Calibri", size: 6.96pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(123.86pt, 614.11pt, "2")
  #set text(font: "Calibri", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(127.34pt, 613.15pt, " as representative donor- and acceptor-like gases demonstrates excellent agreement with ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 626.62pt, "experimental data, indicating the reliability and predictive capabilities of the proposed model. The ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 640.06pt, "methodology can easily be extended to TMDC gas sensors with sensing layers made of semiconducting ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 653.38pt, "TMDCs beyond MoS")
  #set text(font: "Calibri", size: 6.96pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(160.34pt, 654.34pt, "2")
  #set text(font: "Calibri", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(163.82pt, 653.38pt, ". We note, however, that our approach also faces inherent problems. In particular, ")
  #line-at(70.944pt, 666.82pt, "key input parameters required by the model, such as the adsorption energy ")
  #set text(font: "Calibri", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "italic", fill: rgb("#000000"))
